1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,460
Why do you do that?

2
00:00:01,908 --> 00:00:04,088
It's a perfectly ordinary question.

3
00:00:04,168 --> 00:00:06,048
But look at it closely.

4
00:00:06,891 --> 00:00:09,751
One "do" is the thing you're
doing. The other one...

5
00:00:09,981 --> 00:00:11,891
isn't doing anything at all.

6
00:00:12,272 --> 00:00:15,532
Or take "Do you like
coffee?" The verb is "like".

7
00:00:15,872 --> 00:00:18,072
You're asking about liking, not doing.

8
00:00:18,212 --> 00:00:20,272
So what is "do" doing there?

9
00:00:20,779 --> 00:00:24,539
Take it out, and the question
breaks. "Like you coffee?"

10
00:00:25,119 --> 00:00:26,919
Nobody talks like that.

11
00:00:27,450 --> 00:00:30,270
Except... people did. And not just anyone.

12
00:00:30,738 --> 00:00:36,258
"Saw you the weird sisters?" asks
Macbeth, around 1606. No "do".

13
00:00:36,338 --> 00:00:38,298
Just the verb, jumping to the front.

14
00:00:38,623 --> 00:00:40,843
And a few years earlier,
in Hamlet, the same writer

15
00:00:41,073 --> 00:00:44,383
has Polonius ask: "What
do you read, my lord?"

16
00:00:44,563 --> 00:00:46,143
This time, with "do".

17
00:00:46,764 --> 00:00:48,404
Shakespeare used both.

18
00:00:49,004 --> 00:00:52,924
Because in his lifetime, English was
halfway through changing its mind.

19
00:00:53,603 --> 00:00:56,663
In the 1950s, a Swedish
linguist, Alvar Ellegård,

20
00:00:56,743 --> 00:01:00,003
went through centuries of
English texts and counted.

21
00:01:00,863 --> 00:01:04,923
By the 1570s, more than half of
the questions he counted used "do".

22
00:01:05,623 --> 00:01:07,943
In some kinds, three in four.

23
00:01:08,245 --> 00:01:13,325
So this isn't a rule English always
had. It crept in... and took over.

24
00:01:13,525 --> 00:01:14,685
But where did it come from?

25
00:01:14,915 --> 00:01:16,785
And why "do", of all words?

26
00:01:17,275 --> 00:01:20,755
By the way, if you like videos
like this, consider subscribing.

27
00:01:21,375 --> 00:01:22,975
It really helps the channel.

28
00:01:25,365 --> 00:01:28,665
Go back a thousand years, to a
schoolbook for teaching Latin.

29
00:01:29,245 --> 00:01:33,345
Between the Latin lines, someone
wrote in the English of the day.

30
00:01:33,954 --> 00:01:37,554
A teacher asks a ploughman, roughly:
"What sayest thou, ploughman?"

31
00:01:38,701 --> 00:01:40,541
That's how a question worked.

32
00:01:41,021 --> 00:01:43,681
You took the verb and moved
it in front of the subject.

33
00:01:44,521 --> 00:01:46,741
"Thou sayest" became "sayest thou?"

34
00:01:47,355 --> 00:01:49,615
German still does exactly this.

35
00:01:50,195 --> 00:01:52,315
"You like coffee" becomes
"Like you coffee?"

36
00:01:52,395 --> 00:01:55,115
And no German finds that strange.

37
00:01:55,925 --> 00:01:58,205
English kept the old way for centuries.

38
00:01:59,045 --> 00:02:02,785
The King James Bible, in
1611: "Lovest thou me?"

39
00:02:03,505 --> 00:02:05,745
"Understandest thou what thou readest?"

40
00:02:06,065 --> 00:02:08,045
And modern English still does it.

41
00:02:08,465 --> 00:02:12,945
Just not with ordinary verbs.
"Is she happy?" "Can you swim?"

42
00:02:13,325 --> 00:02:14,605
"Have you seen it?"

43
00:02:14,871 --> 00:02:19,911
And when you're angry enough, the old
pattern comes right back. "How dare you?"

44
00:02:20,151 --> 00:02:21,471
Not "How do you dare?"

45
00:02:22,075 --> 00:02:24,815
"Dare" is a very old verb,
from the same odd little family

46
00:02:25,045 --> 00:02:28,855
as "can" and "may", and it never
fully learned the new rule.

47
00:02:31,325 --> 00:02:34,025
Saying "no" has an even stranger history.

48
00:02:34,749 --> 00:02:39,089
In Old English, to say "I don't say",
you put a tiny word in front of the verb.

49
00:02:39,629 --> 00:02:41,629
Literally: "I not say."

50
00:02:42,092 --> 00:02:43,992
But tiny words wear out.

51
00:02:44,512 --> 00:02:46,792
Said quickly, they barely make a sound.

52
00:02:47,192 --> 00:02:48,932
So speakers propped it up.

53
00:02:49,488 --> 00:02:52,588
They added a second word after
the verb, meaning "no whit".

54
00:02:52,668 --> 00:02:55,188
Not a bit. Nothing at all.

55
00:02:55,430 --> 00:02:59,110
That second word shrank over time,
into the word you know as "not".

56
00:02:59,430 --> 00:03:01,890
"Not" started life as "nothing".

57
00:03:02,483 --> 00:03:05,783
For a while, English used
both at once. And then some.

58
00:03:06,503 --> 00:03:09,853
In the 1390s, Chaucer describes a knight.

59
00:03:10,043 --> 00:03:11,543
Roughly, in modern words:

60
00:03:12,225 --> 00:03:16,725
"He never yet no villainy not said, in
all his life, to no kind of person."

61
00:03:17,335 --> 00:03:21,395
Four negatives in one sentence.
And it wasn't bad grammar.

62
00:03:21,895 --> 00:03:23,975
It meant: never, ever, not once.

63
00:03:24,867 --> 00:03:27,387
Then the tiny word in
front faded away, and

64
00:03:27,617 --> 00:03:31,767
"not" was left on its own,
after the verb. "I know not."

65
00:03:32,453 --> 00:03:36,153
That's exactly what Hamlet says to
his mother: "I know not 'seems'."

66
00:03:36,973 --> 00:03:38,853
Same play as "What do you read?"

67
00:03:39,484 --> 00:03:41,104
Next came "I do not know".

68
00:03:41,184 --> 00:03:43,504
And finally, "I don't know."

69
00:03:43,864 --> 00:03:47,644
Each step took centuries, and
nobody planned a single one.

70
00:03:48,139 --> 00:03:49,999
French went round the same loop.

71
00:03:50,399 --> 00:03:54,459
Its word for "not", "pas", originally
meant "a step". "I don't walk...

72
00:03:54,689 --> 00:03:56,099
a single step."

73
00:03:58,329 --> 00:04:00,249
So where does "do" come in?

74
00:04:00,899 --> 00:04:04,299
The first empty "do"s
turn up in the 1200s,

75
00:04:04,379 --> 00:04:06,924
in the west of England, often in verse.

76
00:04:07,225 --> 00:04:10,365
In King Horn, a poem from
the 1200s, a man puts down

77
00:04:10,595 --> 00:04:13,505
his cloak, roughly: "His
cloak he did down lay."

78
00:04:13,585 --> 00:04:16,425
Which simply means: he laid it down.

79
00:04:17,008 --> 00:04:18,908
For a poet, that's handy.

80
00:04:19,648 --> 00:04:23,568
"Did lay" leaves the plain verb at the
end of the line, where it can rhyme.

81
00:04:23,697 --> 00:04:26,097
But that explains why poets liked it...

82
00:04:26,177 --> 00:04:27,757
not where it came from.

83
00:04:28,197 --> 00:04:30,277
And on that, linguists still disagree.

84
00:04:31,091 --> 00:04:35,011
One idea: it grew out of an older "do"
that meant "make something happen".

85
00:04:35,631 --> 00:04:37,691
"He did build a house": he had it built.

86
00:04:37,921 --> 00:04:41,451
Use that often enough, and
the meaning drains away.

87
00:04:42,135 --> 00:04:44,335
Another idea points next door.

88
00:04:44,795 --> 00:04:48,295
Welsh, Cornish and Breton
use "do" in a similar way.

89
00:04:48,867 --> 00:04:52,767
In Breton, "I read" can be,
quite literally, "Reading, I do."

90
00:04:53,337 --> 00:04:57,577
Did English pick the habit up from
its Celtic neighbours? Possibly.

91
00:04:57,957 --> 00:05:01,737
But there's no direct evidence, and
many linguists aren't convinced.

92
00:05:04,427 --> 00:05:06,687
What we do know is what happened next.

93
00:05:07,127 --> 00:05:09,487
Because questions weren't its only home.

94
00:05:10,178 --> 00:05:16,158
In the 1500s, it became far more common
in ordinary statements, too. No emphasis.

95
00:05:16,298 --> 00:05:19,458
No extra meaning. It was just... there.

96
00:05:20,391 --> 00:05:23,891
Here's Genesis, in the King James
Bible: "She took of the fruit thereof,

97
00:05:24,121 --> 00:05:28,511
and did eat, and gave also unto her
husband with her; and he did eat."

98
00:05:29,190 --> 00:05:31,830
Not "she ate". "She did eat."

99
00:05:32,550 --> 00:05:34,750
And William Tyndale's
translation, which the King

100
00:05:34,980 --> 00:05:37,570
James borrowed from heavily,
had simply said "ate".

101
00:05:38,463 --> 00:05:41,503
At its peak, around
1560, roughly one plain

102
00:05:41,733 --> 00:05:44,823
statement in twelve had
this empty "do" in it.

103
00:05:45,343 --> 00:05:51,072
And then it retreated. By the late 1600s,
it was fewer than one in a hundred.

104
00:05:51,372 --> 00:05:55,412
It vanished from plain statements, and
kept only the jobs where it was useful.

105
00:05:55,792 --> 00:05:59,532
Questions. Negatives. And one more.

106
00:06:00,113 --> 00:06:03,673
"Do you actually like
coffee?" "I DO like coffee!"

107
00:06:04,477 --> 00:06:06,557
That's what's left of the empty "do".

108
00:06:06,817 --> 00:06:10,637
In a plain statement, today, it
only turns up to add emphasis.

109
00:06:11,077 --> 00:06:13,837
Which makes this a
perfectly good sentence:

110
00:06:14,647 --> 00:06:16,007
"I DO do that!"

111
00:06:16,487 --> 00:06:18,147
One "do" is the action.

112
00:06:18,627 --> 00:06:20,447
The other insists it's true.

113
00:06:22,877 --> 00:06:24,757
But here's the strangest part.

114
00:06:25,517 --> 00:06:27,557
When linguists plotted how fast "do" was

115
00:06:27,787 --> 00:06:30,157
spreading, in questions
and in negatives...

116
00:06:30,975 --> 00:06:33,575
the lines started at
different heights, but until

117
00:06:33,805 --> 00:06:37,955
about 1575, they all rose
at the same underlying rate.

118
00:06:38,035 --> 00:06:40,875
As if it were one single change.

119
00:06:41,611 --> 00:06:44,591
The most widely accepted
explanation: English

120
00:06:44,821 --> 00:06:47,631
verbs were slowly losing
the ability to move.

121
00:06:48,231 --> 00:06:50,871
In front of the subject,
or in front of "not".

122
00:06:51,244 --> 00:06:53,784
But a question still
needed something up front.

123
00:06:54,504 --> 00:06:57,624
A negative still needed
something to hang "not" on.

124
00:06:58,033 --> 00:06:59,673
And "do" was already there.

125
00:07:00,213 --> 00:07:03,173
Short, common, and by now
meaning almost nothing.

126
00:07:03,784 --> 00:07:06,204
It's an explanation that fits the numbers.

127
00:07:06,884 --> 00:07:09,164
The texts show what changed, not why.

128
00:07:12,224 --> 00:07:17,064
By around 1700, the new rule had
mostly won in yes-or-no questions.

129
00:07:18,064 --> 00:07:20,424
Negatives took most of another century.

130
00:07:21,069 --> 00:07:22,829
No committee chose it.

131
00:07:23,209 --> 00:07:25,209
No grammar book introduced it.

132
00:07:25,789 --> 00:07:28,869
The grammar books only described
it, after it had happened.

133
00:07:29,510 --> 00:07:32,630
It grew out of centuries of people
simply talking to each other.

134
00:07:32,710 --> 00:07:35,250
Which is all grammar ever is.

135
00:07:37,500 --> 00:07:40,940
So the next time someone asks
you, "Why do you do that?"

136
00:07:41,628 --> 00:07:43,948
One "do" is the thing you actually do.

137
00:07:44,528 --> 00:07:46,168
The other is a habit that's been spreading

138
00:07:46,398 --> 00:07:49,408
for seven hundred years,
still doing its job.

139
00:07:50,003 --> 00:07:52,423
And if that seems
needlessly complicated...

140
00:07:52,723 --> 00:07:54,413
do you really think English cares?
